00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:41.320
the, sort of, the
core four-person team

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:44.330
that created this group
in the first place.

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:46.720
We were actually
working on Stackdriver.

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:49.710
We were part of the
original cloud DevOps group.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:52.390
So Stackdriver was a
Google acquisition,

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:55.150
I would say, maybe
five or six years ago,

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:58.240
and they do a lot
of monitoring--

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.850
monitoring different
services, servers--

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:03.760
so you can imagine, there's
a lot of data visualization

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:05.080
elements to it.

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:07.150
Many complex
different dashboards

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:11.680
using line charts, bar charts,
and so on to visualize, again,

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:14.420
services, infrastructures,
and so on.

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:17.970
So we, being part of
that original team,

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:20.390
were already very compelled
to be in that space.

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:23.580
We are all very passionate
about data visualization.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:26.260
But at some moment
in time, we felt

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:29.570
there was a huge demand
for more complex,

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:32.540
sophisticated visualizations,
and not just by Stackdriver

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:35.270
alone, but many other teams.

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:37.570
So what I did is that
I went across Google

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:41.390
and talked to various teams
that were doing related work.

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:43.570
And there was, again,
a lot of demand

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:46.420
for this type of thinking,
this really sophisticated, more

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:48.880
advanced visualization models.

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:51.550
And you can think about
this not as your typical bar

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:54.490
chart or your pie
chart, but something

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:57.730
like a natural visualization,
something like a really

00:07:57.730 --> 00:07:59.655
sophisticated timeline.

00:07:59.655 --> 00:08:01.030
These are not the
things that are

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:03.850
available at the simplest
data visualization

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:06.100
toolkits available out there.

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:08.800
So the demand was definitely
there. however, there was not

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:11.500
a lot of teams able to
build it as we wanted,

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:13.240
as we needed for GCP.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:16.510
So, I was able to convince
our leadership that this

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:19.640
was a huge sort of opportunity.

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:21.920
So I was able to build
that team from scratch.

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:26.620
And I think now we are around
14 people, and a mix of UX

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:30.490
designers, researchers,
UX engineers, essentially

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:32.710
prototypers, and
software engineers

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:34.720
actually building
some of the components

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:35.877
that we are creating.

00:08:35.877 --> 00:08:36.669
JON FOUST: Awesome.

00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:38.553
So you said something
very interesting.

00:08:38.553 --> 00:08:39.970
You had to convince
the leadership

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:41.350
that this was very important.

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:44.590
So, can you tell our listeners
why data visualization

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:45.682
is so important?

00:08:45.682 --> 00:08:47.390
MANUEL LIMA: I like
to think about this--

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:49.930
one way is called the
understanding spectrum.

00:08:49.930 --> 00:08:53.380
This is how data converts
into information,

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:55.780
information converts
into knowledge,

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:57.820
and knowledge
converts into wisdom.

00:08:57.820 --> 00:09:00.190
This is the understanding
spectrum, normally.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:02.470
Now, data visualization
really comes in

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:05.770
in building a bridge between
information and knowledge,

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:07.330
which is also the hardest part.

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:10.540
Information surrounds
us every single day,

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:12.970
and our ability to actually
generate data and information

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:15.850
has, by far, outpaced our
ability to actually make

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:17.520
sense of that data.

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:19.520
And that's where data
visualization can come in.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:22.330
It's almost a lens
for understanding.

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:25.300
It allows us to understand
in a graphical way a lot

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:27.910
of the hidden patterns in
the data and in information

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:29.660
that you wouldn't see otherwise.

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:32.830
So that's the metaphor of, like,
making the invisible visible

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:34.162
is so powerful.

00:09:34.162 --> 00:09:35.870
GABI FERRARA: So, if
you can tell us what

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:37.948
your team been up to lately.

