00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:03.070
or they are building this
sophisticated dashboard.

00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:06.340
Some of these challenges
are external-facing that are

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:07.680
customers outside--

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:10.000
let's say Google
Analytics, for example,

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:12.190
but other are internal.

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:14.290
We have a lot of
internal dashboards,

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:16.300
a lot of internal
tools that deal

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:18.820
with really sophisticated
ways of visualizing data

00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:19.990
and information.

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:21.550
And we come in as consultants.

00:10:21.550 --> 00:10:22.580
We support them.

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:24.550
We help them achieve a
better product overall.

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:27.010
And also, of course, we
provide a lot of UX expertise.

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:29.680
So we help with usability,
with accessibility,

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:31.130
and things of that nature.

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:34.450
We are also very passionate
about dataviz and teaching

00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:37.900
dataviz, so we organize things
like an internal speaker

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:40.900
series, where people come
in and talk about the work.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:44.860
We teach data visualization in
this internal almost university

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:46.420
that we have at Google.

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:49.490
We teach other Googlers
on data visualization.

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:51.670
But the core of
what we do is really

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:55.720
building cloud charts, which
is a set of data visualization

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:58.000
components so that
other teams can actually

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:00.820
reuse them in an easy way.

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:02.860
That's the core of our business.

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:06.820
That's where most of us
are really working hard on.

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:10.510
And a secondary pillar
that we are trying to build

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:14.350
is related to design
ops, design operations.

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:17.360
And we are building
some tools and apps

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:20.150
data visualization related
to our part of teams,

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:23.880
be more effective, collaborate
better, and just raise

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:27.000
the quality of their output.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:28.000
GABI FERRARA: You said--

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:28.630
sorry--

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:29.180
MANUEL LIMA: Yes.

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:30.680
GABI FERRARA: --just
to go back, you

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:32.270
said, like, teach
a lot of people

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:33.485
about data visualization?

00:11:33.485 --> 00:11:34.360
MANUEL LIMA: Correct.

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:36.027
GABI FERRARA: How
much math do you need?

00:11:36.027 --> 00:11:36.902
MANUEL LIMA: [LAUGHS]

00:11:36.902 --> 00:11:38.360
GABI FERRARA: Like,
math-- like, I

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:40.070
don't do math for a long time.

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:40.820
MANUEL LIMA: Yeah.

00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:42.770
That's a common
misunderstanding,

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:43.370
I have to say.

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:45.350
Like, it was kind
of like that, maybe,

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:47.280
I would say, 10 to 15 years ago.

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:50.240
If you wanted to do any
type of substantial work

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:53.880
in data visualization, you
would have to be good at math

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:56.330
and also have a good
understanding of a programming

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:57.290
language.

00:11:57.290 --> 00:11:59.000
These days, you've had--

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:02.230
there was this democratization
of visualization tools,

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:05.630
so it's pretty easy
for many other people

00:12:05.630 --> 00:12:08.150
without that expertise,
without that knowledge,

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:11.912
to do some really substantial
work in this domain.

00:12:11.912 --> 00:12:14.370
And I think that that's been
really productive because that

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:17.000
has allowed the field to
grow immensely and include

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.653
many different people from
many different disciplines.

00:12:19.653 --> 00:12:20.570
GABI FERRARA: Awesome.

00:12:20.570 --> 00:12:22.580
So, you've mentioned that you
worked on a bunch of tooling,

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:23.840
and you work with
a bunch of teams,

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:25.190
and you do a lot of courses.

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:27.440
So I'm curious,
since the team was

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:30.770
founded by the four of you, what
has really come out of the data

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:33.740
visualization team that you
can puff your chest out about?

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:36.540
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:37.760
MANUEL LIMA: That's good.

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:39.630
I mean, staffing the
team was really fun.

00:12:39.630 --> 00:12:42.650
I'm, of course, a UX manager,
so I love growing teams,

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:44.870
you know, finding
the right culture,

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:46.760
growing them in a
balanced way, making sure

00:12:46.760 --> 00:12:50.150
that we have the right
cross-discipline ratios,

00:12:50.150 --> 00:12:53.690
and making sure we are building
the right talent in part

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:54.915
of that group of people.

00:12:54.915 --> 00:12:55.540
So I love that.

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:58.160
That is one of the things that
I feel most proud of, just

00:12:58.160 --> 00:12:59.582
building our team from scratch.

00:12:59.582 --> 00:13:01.040
With the right
capability, everyone

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:04.748
being so passionate for this
domain just definitely helps.

00:13:04.748 --> 00:13:07.040
And I feel this is also
something that I carried around

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:07.800
with my career.

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:10.070
I believe that
passion is a magnet.

00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:13.370
It contaminates others and it
allows you to do a lot more.

00:13:13.370 --> 00:13:15.590
And I feel like we
have done so much

00:13:15.590 --> 00:13:17.450
with a small group
of people because we

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:21.170
have that drive, because we
have that huge passion for data

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:21.853
visualization.

00:13:21.853 --> 00:13:23.270
But one of the
things that I feel,

00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:26.540
again, is staffing the
team, but also reaching out.

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:29.690
A lot of times, we do amazing
work internally at Google,

00:13:29.690 --> 00:13:31.910
but we don't actually
go outside and actually

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:35.390
talk to other people and teach
them as well what we're doing.

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:38.600
So, a few months back, we
partnered with the Material

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:42.800
Team and we actually launched
externally for everyone to use.

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:44.510
So everyone that's
listening to us

00:13:44.510 --> 00:13:48.200
can actually go and see a set
of data visualization guidelines

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:50.990
that we did in partnership
with Material, detailing things

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:53.240
like the graphical language
that you should use,

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:55.790
what type of charts you
need for a given situation--

00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:57.740
a lot of really
interesting principles.

00:13:57.740 --> 00:13:59.870
And this has been
extremely successful.

00:13:59.870 --> 00:14:02.510
I think right now that
Material section has

00:14:02.510 --> 00:14:08.150
been seen by around 100,000
unique users and the principles

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:10.100
that we published
on Medium has been

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:12.110
seen by roughly 60,000 people.

00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:14.720
There's definitely a lot of
appetite, a lot of interest,

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:16.280
for this type of
material, and I'm

00:14:16.280 --> 00:14:20.660
really proud for us to be
reaching out to our users

00:14:20.660 --> 00:14:21.938
outside of Google.

00:14:21.938 --> 00:14:22.730
JON FOUST: Awesome.

00:14:22.730 --> 00:14:24.730
So I'm going to circle
back to about the tooling

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:26.563
that you've mentioned
and a lot of the teams

00:14:26.563 --> 00:14:27.410
that you work with.

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:29.720
For your team, you're all
very passionate about data

00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:30.390
visualization.

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:34.730
But curious, does everybody
need to be an expert on data

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:37.595
or just be passionate about
it to actually get into this?

00:14:37.595 --> 00:14:38.970
MANUEL LIMA: That's
a good point.

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:41.150
I think passion helps,
but we have had people

00:14:41.150 --> 00:14:44.000
joining the team that didn't
have a solid background

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:45.430
or expertise in this space.

00:14:45.430 --> 00:14:47.540
They were, like, just
tremendous researchers

00:14:47.540 --> 00:14:51.770
or tremendous designers and they
just had that appetite for it.

00:14:51.770 --> 00:14:54.380
So, I think you can definitely
excel, but again, in my view,

00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:56.270
passion goes a long way.

00:14:56.270 --> 00:15:01.370
It just allows you to that
extra 10% of dedication.

00:15:01.370 --> 00:15:04.160
GABI FERRARA: So once
you build this, basically

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:07.960
a set of guidelines
for data visualization

00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:12.110
that people will use
across different teams.

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:16.100
How hard was it to build
something like that, because

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:19.070
like the fact that you said
you're a horizontal team,

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:22.650
there is a kind of uniformity
to everything that you're doing,

00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:25.550
and so, it's more common
across the product.

00:15:25.550 --> 00:15:29.857
And how do you work to
maintain that kind of identity?

00:15:29.857 --> 00:15:31.940
MANUEL LIMA: I think that's
one of the challenges.

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:34.057
And I'm sure the
audience is probably

00:15:34.057 --> 00:15:35.390
familiar with the Material team.

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:37.910
The Material team is
this internal group

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:40.250
that really strives
to build consistency

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:44.240
in terms of the UI and UX across
our various products, Google

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:47.610
products, which is a huge
challenge on its own right.

00:15:47.610 --> 00:15:50.990
So we, in some ways, are
a smaller Material team

00:15:50.990 --> 00:15:53.540
but just focused on
data visualization.

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:57.512
So, we have the same challenge,
albeit a much smaller team.

00:15:57.512 --> 00:15:58.220
But you're right.

00:15:58.220 --> 00:15:59.428
I think it comes in two ways.

00:15:59.428 --> 00:16:02.840
One is the guidelines,
and then the other one

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:06.200
is providing the specific
components that others can use.

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:08.150
And they kind of
go hand in hand.

00:16:08.150 --> 00:16:12.140
On the guidelines, the work
that we did started earlier on,

00:16:12.140 --> 00:16:15.710
I would say maybe 18 months ago
or so-- or even two years ago.

00:16:15.710 --> 00:16:18.920
The first time is when a
group of us across Google

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:19.890
got together--

00:16:19.890 --> 00:16:22.410
I think we did a couple
of sprints on this--

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:24.140
and this was a
really wide effort.

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:27.550
This was, I think, 12 different
product areas across Google,

00:16:27.550 --> 00:16:31.960
from Google Analytics, Search,
Google Cloud, all these PAs,

00:16:31.960 --> 00:16:33.220
that we call internally.

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:36.730
So, all these designers from all
these groups getting together

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:40.120
and thinking about what would
the right set of guidelines

00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:42.400
that we could provide
in a unified way,

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:44.290
so that everyone
else on the team

00:16:44.290 --> 00:16:46.007
could build the
right type of chart?

00:16:46.007 --> 00:16:47.590
At the time, we were
not even thinking

00:16:47.590 --> 00:16:49.810
about providing a
specific component

00:16:49.810 --> 00:16:51.910
that you could actually use.

00:16:51.910 --> 00:16:53.680
We were just a
set of guidelines.

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:57.340
And that became our internal
Google dataviz spec.

00:16:57.340 --> 00:16:59.500
This is again
available internally

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:03.190
and the one that we ended up
publishing by the Material team

00:17:03.190 --> 00:17:05.750
externally is just
a subset of that.

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:07.579
So internally,
it's a bit heavier.

00:17:07.579 --> 00:17:12.849
We cover almost everything from
motion design to interaction.

00:17:12.849 --> 00:17:15.940
We cover aspects of this on
the external one as well,

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:18.849
but the internal is, by
far, more comprehensive.

00:17:18.849 --> 00:17:20.339
That was a really fun project.

00:17:20.339 --> 00:17:22.839
That was a really fun sort of
initiative to be part of that,

00:17:22.839 --> 00:17:26.270
and that has been immensely
helpful for, again,

00:17:26.270 --> 00:17:28.720
many different designers,
engineers, and researchers

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:30.620
across the entire company.

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:33.230
So that was on the
guidelines aspect of it.

00:17:33.230 --> 00:17:35.710
And then, part of that
we are doing right now

00:17:35.710 --> 00:17:38.380
is releasing
specific components.

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:40.270
We are building a
library, a toolkit,

00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:43.750
So that other engineers across
multiple different areas

00:17:43.750 --> 00:17:44.810
as well.

00:17:44.810 --> 00:17:47.870
And of course, most
importantly, Cloud and GCP

00:17:47.870 --> 00:17:49.800
can leverage those components.

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:51.550
And consistency, to
your point, Gabrielle,

00:17:51.550 --> 00:17:53.800
is critical because that's
one of the reasons why

00:17:53.800 --> 00:17:55.540
we are doing this,
why we are building

00:17:55.540 --> 00:17:59.140
this library in the first place
is that users don't get to see

00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:01.000
a different bar
chart, a different pie

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:03.460
chart in every single product
that they navigate through

00:18:03.460 --> 00:18:04.780
within GCP.

00:18:04.780 --> 00:18:06.040
That wouldn't make sense.

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:08.830
Consistency builds familiarity
and it's pretty important

00:18:08.830 --> 00:18:11.950
that people and users recognize
the same type of experience,

00:18:11.950 --> 00:18:13.728
the same type of
charting mechanism,

00:18:13.728 --> 00:18:15.520
across the different
products that they use

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:17.062
and navigate to
achieve a given task.

00:18:17.062 --> 00:18:19.353
GABI FERRARA: So you brought
up this component library,

00:18:19.353 --> 00:18:20.290
and I'm curious.

00:18:20.290 --> 00:18:22.920
Of course, it's used widely
across Google internally,

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:25.720
but what would you say is the
biggest advantage of actually

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:26.590
building this out.

00:18:26.590 --> 00:18:29.350
Internally of course, things
that happen internally

00:18:29.350 --> 00:18:32.470
have extreme external
benefits for our users,

00:18:32.470 --> 00:18:33.550
so I'm just curious.

00:18:33.550 --> 00:18:35.633
What would you say is the
biggest advantage for us

00:18:35.633 --> 00:18:36.140
internally?

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:37.990
MANUEL LIMA: One is
definitely building

00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:40.600
some of the internal expertise
on this domain, which is, again

00:18:40.600 --> 00:18:42.700
still fairly new to people.

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:44.330
They understand,
maybe, the benefit,

00:18:44.330 --> 00:18:46.360
but they don't
understand how critical

00:18:46.360 --> 00:18:49.000
it can be for even product,
even an internal product.

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:51.250
So, I think it's kind
of raising awareness,

00:18:51.250 --> 00:18:55.340
and education across the
company is a really good effort.

00:18:55.340 --> 00:18:59.170
The benefits of building a
library, per se, is multifold.

00:18:59.170 --> 00:19:00.850
You can increase
velocity because you

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:02.748
don't have to reinvent
the wheel every time.

00:19:02.748 --> 00:19:04.540
You can just pick up
the component and kind

00:19:04.540 --> 00:19:05.450
of plug and play.

00:19:05.450 --> 00:19:08.320
You can also minimize
a lot of the UI,

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:10.180
sort of, complexity
because you end up

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:13.390
creating auxiliary
type of UI components

00:19:13.390 --> 00:19:15.100
that are not
productive at times.

00:19:15.100 --> 00:19:17.050
I think you can really
increase quality--

00:19:17.050 --> 00:19:20.350
optimize UI quality,
consistency, and effectiveness.

00:19:20.350 --> 00:19:21.490
We talk about consistency.

00:19:21.490 --> 00:19:23.200
It helps build familiarity.

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:25.900
It helps that you find
the same type of chart

00:19:25.900 --> 00:19:27.945
every time you navigate
to a different product.

00:19:27.945 --> 00:19:30.070
And I think it can be a
huge driver for innovation.

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:34.840
Impressing our customers and
be, really, a differentiator

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:36.520
for GCP, as well.

00:19:36.520 --> 00:19:39.160
GABI FERRARA: You said that
you have a set of guidelines

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:42.520
and that you build
first internally.

00:19:42.520 --> 00:19:44.830
So we're kind of
like the Formula 1--

00:19:44.830 --> 00:19:46.680
cutting edge
internally, and then you

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:51.260
put it outside if it is OK, and
safe, and that kind of stuff.

00:19:51.260 --> 00:19:54.250
So how is the process to
publishing these guidelines

00:19:54.250 --> 00:19:55.990
for the outside world?

00:19:55.990 --> 00:19:58.120
MANUEL LIMA: It was
easier in the sense

00:19:58.120 --> 00:20:01.060
that we had a body of
content already to rely on.

00:20:01.060 --> 00:20:03.040
We didn't invent
everything from scratch,

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:05.590
even though we ended up creating
a bunch of new content just

00:20:05.590 --> 00:20:08.110
for that external
publishing alone.

00:20:08.110 --> 00:20:10.300
But the process was really
a strong partnership

00:20:10.300 --> 00:20:11.260
with the Material team.

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:13.093
We have been very close
since the beginning,

00:20:13.093 --> 00:20:15.910
since the inception
of our group,

00:20:15.910 --> 00:20:19.150
working very closely with
them reviewing content

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:20.170
several times.

00:20:20.170 --> 00:20:22.840
And they are very, of course,
familiar with the process.

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:27.430
And again, we actually published
under the material.io website

00:20:27.430 --> 00:20:31.870
so they are very familiar with
the whole process of iterating

00:20:31.870 --> 00:20:34.670
on content,
publishing, and so on.

00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:36.760
So that was usually helpful.

00:20:36.760 --> 00:20:39.190
And then also, we
published under the Google

00:20:39.190 --> 00:20:42.477
Design on Medium,
and that's also

00:20:42.477 --> 00:20:44.560
co-owned by the Material
team, so that partnership

00:20:44.560 --> 00:20:46.900
was fundamental, I would say.

00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:48.340
And in case you
might be wondering

00:20:48.340 --> 00:20:50.825
about what those
guidelines can be,

00:20:50.825 --> 00:20:52.450
we kind of started
off with principles.

00:20:52.450 --> 00:20:55.433
Principles are things that
can be fundamental for users

00:20:55.433 --> 00:20:57.100
to understand-- how
do they actually get

00:20:57.100 --> 00:20:59.380
started with data
visualization, right?

00:20:59.380 --> 00:21:02.338
Principles might be used
for you to either evaluate

00:21:02.338 --> 00:21:04.630
a specific data visualization
work, a chart that you're

00:21:04.630 --> 00:21:07.840
working on, or even before
you even start doing anything,

00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:13.450
just to start inspiring and
scaffoling the whole project.

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:15.460
So one of the principles
we have, for example,

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:16.990
is "be honest."

00:21:16.990 --> 00:21:19.300
Data accuracy and
integrity come first.

00:21:19.300 --> 00:21:23.080
Don't destroy or confuse
information for embellishment

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:24.220
or partiality.

00:21:24.220 --> 00:21:26.870
As with any form of
communication, charts--

00:21:26.870 --> 00:21:29.090
data visualization-- can
be used for good or bad.

00:21:29.090 --> 00:21:33.200
So being honest, being accurate
is really critical for us

00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:35.360
what we do as Google.

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:37.130
Another principle,
just as an example,

00:21:37.130 --> 00:21:38.960
is "lend a helping hand."

00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:41.780
A lot of the charts that we do,
a lot of the components we do,

00:21:41.780 --> 00:21:43.640
can be fairly complex
to understand.

00:21:43.640 --> 00:21:46.370
You know, it requires a
substantial learning curve.

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:48.860
So, finding ways of
lending a helping hand,

00:21:48.860 --> 00:21:50.810
providing the context and help--

00:21:50.810 --> 00:21:53.360
users navigating the
data is really important.

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:55.790
Building the right
visual, for instance--

00:21:55.790 --> 00:21:57.980
that can comfort
users that this is

00:21:57.980 --> 00:22:00.650
something that's doable, that
they can learn in fast way.

00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:03.320
GABI FERRARA: These principles--
they sound very, very similar

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:05.393
to a lot of our core
values at Google.

00:22:05.393 --> 00:22:07.310
So I'm just curious, how
did these come about?

00:22:07.310 --> 00:22:09.102
MANUEL LIMA: So, the
principles were really

00:22:09.102 --> 00:22:10.190
an interesting project.

00:22:10.190 --> 00:22:12.920
A lot of that work was
from that original team

00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:16.010
of designers across Google,
those 12 different product

00:22:16.010 --> 00:22:18.650
areas that we got
together in a few sprints.

00:22:18.650 --> 00:22:20.010
That was the genesis of it.

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:22.110
We then iterated a few times.

00:22:22.110 --> 00:22:25.100
We also talked to a lot
of different areas, even

00:22:25.100 --> 00:22:27.290
the hardware team at Google.

00:22:27.290 --> 00:22:29.150
They have their own
set of principles.

00:22:29.150 --> 00:22:30.890
We have our own
brand principles.

00:22:30.890 --> 00:22:32.750
There's multiple
instances within Google

00:22:32.750 --> 00:22:35.000
that have their own sort
of specific principles,

00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:37.820
but there are a few ones that
are really common and shared

00:22:37.820 --> 00:22:39.693
across the company.

00:22:39.693 --> 00:22:41.360
So we talked to a
bunch of those people.

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:42.860
We also use research.

00:22:42.860 --> 00:22:45.530
Research has been
really critical for us.

00:22:45.530 --> 00:22:49.610
Very recently, we just
published internally a report

00:22:49.610 --> 00:22:53.600
on data visualization needs
of 33 different product teams

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:55.910
within GCP, by far the
most comprehensive research

00:22:55.910 --> 00:22:57.500
study we ever conducted.

00:22:57.500 --> 00:22:59.510
And through a lot
of that research,

00:22:59.510 --> 00:23:02.030
we kind of add a really
solid understanding

00:23:02.030 --> 00:23:04.850
of what users actually wanted,
what our customers needed

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:06.500
pertaining to data
visualization, what

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:08.370
was really important to them.

00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:11.510
And all those things put
together, we just mix it up,

00:23:11.510 --> 00:23:15.440
and we generate a final list of
six guiding principles that you

00:23:15.440 --> 00:23:17.480
can find on our Medium post.

00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:19.550
And I believe we can
actually serve as a link

00:23:19.550 --> 00:23:22.897
to that article on the
GCP podcasts on the show.

00:23:22.897 --> 00:23:24.480
GABI FERRARA: On the
show notes, yeah.

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:26.990
So, you've written
this article and it's

00:23:26.990 --> 00:23:28.880
been amplified outside.

00:23:28.880 --> 00:23:30.807
Apparently this is
something new to me--

00:23:30.807 --> 00:23:33.140
"Fast Company" actually wrote
an article about the work.

00:23:33.140 --> 00:23:35.893
So I'm curious, how
has this been received?

00:23:35.893 --> 00:23:36.660
MANUEL LIMA: Yeah.

00:23:36.660 --> 00:23:39.077
I mean, my understanding it
has been pretty well received.

00:23:39.077 --> 00:23:42.920
Again, like, we have had
close to 100,000 unique views

00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:46.342
on the Material section that
you can find on material.io,

00:23:46.342 --> 00:23:48.860
under communication,
and then a whole section

00:23:48.860 --> 00:23:50.450
on data visualization.

00:23:50.450 --> 00:23:53.270
And then, I've been following
on social media, it's been,

00:23:53.270 --> 00:23:55.070
you know, especially
after "Fast Company"

00:23:55.070 --> 00:23:57.630
and a few other media
outlets talked about it,

00:23:57.630 --> 00:23:59.870
I think it's been
pretty well received.

00:23:59.870 --> 00:24:01.370
People are really
interested in what

00:24:01.370 --> 00:24:05.000
Google has to say pertaining
to data visualization.

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:08.030
And there was also the Medium
post which got a lot of buzz

00:24:08.030 --> 00:24:08.782
on the principles.

00:24:08.782 --> 00:24:10.490
And I think that people
tend to gravitate

00:24:10.490 --> 00:24:13.010
towards the principles because
they are not highly specific.

00:24:13.010 --> 00:24:16.130
They are just more
generative than prescriptive,

00:24:16.130 --> 00:24:18.777
and I think that resonates
with people a lot more.

00:24:18.777 --> 00:24:21.360
And we are thinking about doing
enhancements also, by the way,

00:24:21.360 --> 00:24:23.720
to those guidelines on
the material.io site.

00:24:23.720 --> 00:24:27.650
Right now, there's a lot of
content on the types of charts

00:24:27.650 --> 00:24:28.550
that you might pick.

00:24:28.550 --> 00:24:30.110
Sometimes you have
a given data set,

00:24:30.110 --> 00:24:32.673
but you don't really know what
chart you should be using,

00:24:32.673 --> 00:24:34.340
so we provide a lot
of guidance on that.

00:24:34.340 --> 00:24:37.700
We also provide guidance
on the graphic language

00:24:37.700 --> 00:24:38.840
that you need to build.

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:42.140
So, how to use color, how to
use typography, how to use

00:24:42.140 --> 00:24:45.330
shape to convey a
given sort of concept.

00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:46.950
So we provide a lot of guidance.

00:24:46.950 --> 00:24:50.750
And I think people have found
that to be extremely useful.

00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:53.870
That level of specificity
that we also provide--

00:24:53.870 --> 00:24:56.040
not just the principles,
high-level principles,

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:57.920
but also like the
do's and dont's--

00:24:57.920 --> 00:25:00.110
what to do and what not to do--

00:25:00.110 --> 00:25:02.000
on specific areas
of building a chart

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:03.985
and building a
data visualization.

00:25:03.985 --> 00:25:06.110
GABI FERRARA: Based on
those guidelines you posted,

00:25:06.110 --> 00:25:08.240
those principles
that you mentioned,

00:25:08.240 --> 00:25:12.980
is there any outside company
or anyone else outside Google

00:25:12.980 --> 00:25:16.070
that you see successfully
implementing this, or in a way

00:25:16.070 --> 00:25:17.840
that you didn't
thought, even, that was

00:25:17.840 --> 00:25:20.180
possible to be able to do it?

00:25:20.180 --> 00:25:21.890
MANUEL LIMA:
Unfortunately, I haven't--

00:25:21.890 --> 00:25:25.220
I haven't actually
been contacted or seen

00:25:25.220 --> 00:25:27.020
anything of that nature.

00:25:27.020 --> 00:25:29.990
It's more like the, kind of,
people reaching out to me

00:25:29.990 --> 00:25:32.390
and saying, hey,
this was very useful.

00:25:32.390 --> 00:25:34.880
We are using it in a given way.

00:25:34.880 --> 00:25:38.360
But I don't think I've actually
seen tangible output influenced

00:25:38.360 --> 00:25:39.830
by our guidelines.

00:25:39.830 --> 00:25:42.036
But maybe it's just
a matter of time.

00:25:42.036 --> 00:25:44.275
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:44.275 --> 00:25:45.650
JON FOUST: We
would like to think

00:25:45.650 --> 00:25:47.490
Manuel Lima for joining us.

00:25:47.490 --> 00:25:48.682
It's been a pleasure.

00:25:48.682 --> 00:25:50.390
With him, right now,
currently recording,

00:25:50.390 --> 00:25:53.390
you can see how very passionate
he is about data visualization.

00:25:53.390 --> 00:25:55.400
So it's great to see
how you've really

00:25:55.400 --> 00:25:57.680
tried to impact
internally with Google,

00:25:57.680 --> 00:26:00.380
and also sharing those
guidelines and principles

00:26:00.380 --> 00:26:01.640
with our external audiences.

00:26:01.640 --> 00:26:02.720
So that's perfect.

00:26:02.720 --> 00:26:06.187
So, we'll be sure to share
