[LAUGHTER]

00:26:24.865 --> 00:26:27.240
GABI FERRARA: So, back to the
question of the week, Jon--

00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:35.620
what are the most common
products used in cloud gaming?

00:26:35.620 --> 00:26:37.370
JON FOUST: I'll probably
go over a couple,

00:26:37.370 --> 00:26:41.210
but one product that I've seen a
big shift to was Cloud Spanner.

00:26:41.210 --> 00:26:43.520
Customers have been using
Cloud Spanner to store, like,

00:26:43.520 --> 00:26:46.640
player authentication data and
inventories, or pretty much

00:26:46.640 --> 00:26:48.020
any long-term state storage.

00:26:48.020 --> 00:26:49.970
So, the great
thing about it it's

00:26:49.970 --> 00:26:52.380
relatively quick, scalable,
and highly available,

00:26:52.380 --> 00:26:55.730
so there's been definitely been
a big shift to Cloud Spanner.

00:26:55.730 --> 00:26:58.240
Another product that's been
used quite often is Redis,

00:26:58.240 --> 00:27:00.740
one of the open source projects
that we work on, Open Match,

00:27:00.740 --> 00:27:02.690
comes default with
Redis, and it's

00:27:02.690 --> 00:27:05.630
used for state storage
or caching player

00:27:05.630 --> 00:27:09.410
data, which allows the
framework to actually access

00:27:09.410 --> 00:27:11.930
that data relatively
quickly and creates

00:27:11.930 --> 00:27:14.280
very competitive
matches very quickly.

00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:16.250
So, that's also another
part of the [INAUDIBLE]

00:27:16.250 --> 00:27:17.870
seeing a big shift towards.

00:27:17.870 --> 00:27:19.820
And it's widely
known that VMs have

00:27:19.820 --> 00:27:22.430
been used as a product
for game servers,

00:27:22.430 --> 00:27:24.470
but there's been a big
shift to Kubernetes.

00:27:24.470 --> 00:27:27.260
Big shout-out to Mark
in the Agones project.

00:27:27.260 --> 00:27:28.850
I plug him every week I can.

00:27:28.850 --> 00:27:29.960
[LAUGHS]

00:27:29.960 --> 00:27:32.300
But there's been a big
shift to using Kubernetes

00:27:32.300 --> 00:27:35.000
because it's highly scalable
and highly available.

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:38.240
And the great thing about
it is that it auto-scales.

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:40.430
And I think that
whenever one of your pods

00:27:40.430 --> 00:27:42.410
goes down and always
spins right back up,

00:27:42.410 --> 00:27:45.320
the product, in itself,
just speaks to the power

00:27:45.320 --> 00:27:46.610
that it would have in gaming.

00:27:46.610 --> 00:27:48.380
And the last product
that I've been

00:27:48.380 --> 00:27:51.500
using personally quite a
bit for my personal project

00:27:51.500 --> 00:27:52.370
is Pub / Sub.

00:27:52.370 --> 00:27:54.890
I've been using it a lot
for messaging, as well

00:27:54.890 --> 00:27:58.980
as sending data relatively
quickly between two clients.

00:27:58.980 --> 00:28:02.720
So Pub / Sub is great
because, if done right,

00:28:02.720 --> 00:28:06.330
you should have real-time
messaging between two clients.

00:28:06.330 --> 00:28:08.690
So if you're interested,
feel free to check out

00:28:08.690 --> 00:28:11.270
all of the links for all of
these products in our show

00:28:11.270 --> 00:28:12.062
notes

00:28:12.062 --> 00:28:12.770
GABI FERRARA: OK.

00:28:12.770 --> 00:28:16.160
Just reminding people that
we do have a Cloud Memory

00:28:16.160 --> 00:28:18.020
Storage, which is
Redis compatible,

00:28:18.020 --> 00:28:19.362
so you can be using that, too.

00:28:19.362 --> 00:28:21.320
JON FOUST: And the great
thing about Open Match

00:28:21.320 --> 00:28:23.300
is that Redis just
comes default,

00:28:23.300 --> 00:28:25.480
but pretty much any
component can be swapped out.

00:28:25.480 --> 00:28:26.930
So if you want to
use Memorystore,

00:28:26.930 --> 00:28:29.330
if you want to use Firestore,
pretty much anything

00:28:29.330 --> 00:28:32.990
that you want to use for storing
or caching your play data

00:28:32.990 --> 00:28:34.320
you can easily just swap out.

00:28:34.320 --> 00:28:37.610
And that also goes for pretty
much any other component

00:28:37.610 --> 00:28:38.880
of the project as well.

